WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:03.501
-Our next guest is a talented actor you know from shows like

00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:06.567
"That '70s Show" and films such as "BlacKkKlansman."

00:00:06.567 --> 00:00:09.400
He executive-produces and stars in "Home Economics,"

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.200
which airs Wednesdays on ABC.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.067
Let's take a look. -My God. You were right.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.167
Hanging out with people half our age is kinda fun.

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:16.767
I just signed up for student council.

00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:18.767
-Jeremy just asked me to dinner with his grandparents.

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:20.634
I can order anything I want! -And the night is still young.

00:00:20.634 --> 00:00:21.901
-And so are we.

00:00:21.901 --> 00:00:23.300
[ Both screaming ]

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.501
♪♪

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:28.467
-[ Cheers ]

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:32.334
♪♪

00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:33.968
-If you think about it,

00:00:33.968 --> 00:00:35.667
how would we even test simulation theory?

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:38.033
Any sufficiently sophisticated simulation

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:40.133
would be designed to evade such a test.

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:42.200
-Dude, you're scaring me right now.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.133
-Dude, I know.

00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:47.200
-Please welcome to the show Topher Grace, everybody!

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:55.367
♪♪

00:00:55.367 --> 00:01:02.033
♪♪

00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:04.000
Welcome back! -Oh, man, so good to see you.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.200
-It is great to see you, too.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.267
This is a wonderful show about adult siblings

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:09.667
with a complicated relationship.

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:12.000
In that clip, you were not playing a college student.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.067
You were playing -- -Yeah, just to be clear --

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:16.167
Yeah, those days are over. -Yes.

00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:18.267
But this was about people closer to our age

00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:20.400
who are trying to relive their younger days.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.634
-Yes, they're having a tough week, and I go to --

00:01:22.634 --> 00:01:25.100
I'm a novelist, and we have twins, so I'm like --

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.167
I decided to go work at a college, at a library.

00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:30.334
And it's really dim lighting.

00:01:30.334 --> 00:01:31.767
And one of the students is like,

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:33.701
"Hey, you wanna come to this party?"

00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:35.434
And I go, and then my wife comes to stop me,

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:37.200
and then she starts partying.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.100
And spoiler -- they figure it out.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.534
-You hurt yourself in this episode,

00:01:43.534 --> 00:01:44.968
in many of the episodes.

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:47.267
You're an executive producer. Do you ever try to talk them

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.667
out of having your character get injured so often?

00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:51.634
-No, it's the worst, 'cause when I read these --

00:01:51.634 --> 00:01:54.601
I mean, I knew you when I was -- back in the day when I was 25.

00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:56.467
-Yeah, back when you could jump --

00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:58.000
do a broad jump through the sky!

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.567
-That's what I was always doing, as you remember.

00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:01.601
-Yeah, just constantly. -Yeah.

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:05.534
And when I read the scripts, I feel like I'm 25.

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:08.100
I mean, I used to always do this stuff on "'70s Show."

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.334
I remember, between the, like, acts, we'd, like,

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:12.400
be jumping on a trampoline or something.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.667
And then when I do it, like, I broke my ankle.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.367
This was right before one of those things you saw.

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.167
We had to jump out a window. They figure out who we are.

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:21.667
So we literally jump out the window.

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:24.000
And I was like, "Oh, yeah, I can stick that landing."

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.467
And then, luckily, it was Christmas break afterward,

00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:28.934
so I had like 18 days to, like, heal up.

00:02:28.934 --> 00:02:30.467
-Did you -- When you broke it, was --

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:32.033
-I didn't break it. I didn't break it.

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:33.367
-Okay, good. Thank goodness.

00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:35.601
'Cause I was gonna say 18 days doesn't seem like enough.

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:37.234
-Yeah, but that's the kind of actor I am.

00:02:37.234 --> 00:02:38.868
-Yeah, that's right. -Push through it, yeah.

00:02:38.868 --> 00:02:40.434
-You've always been. Hey, you know, so, we --

00:02:40.434 --> 00:02:42.067
You talk about the fact that we knew each other.

00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:46.234
I knew you when you were cast as Venom in one of the previous --

00:02:46.234 --> 00:02:47.968
-Wait. Can I tell the story when we first met?

00:02:47.968 --> 00:02:49.300
-Yeah, please. -It's my favorite.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.000
I was in a movie with Dennis Quaid.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.634
-Yeah. -And you came by,

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:54.868
and I knew who you were.

00:02:54.868 --> 00:02:56.300
I was a big fan of yours from "SNL."

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.000
You weren't even doing "Update" yet.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.667
And we were talking, and Dennis Quaid --

00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:03.100
And this is really Dennis Quaid's heyday.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.701
And you -- He said --

00:03:04.701 --> 00:03:07.100
You said, "Hi, Dennis. Nice to meet you."

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:11.167
And he said, "No, we've met before."

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:15.334
And you said, "I feel like I would've remembered."

00:03:15.334 --> 00:03:17.267
-Right. It was insulting --

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.133
I was trying to stress to him, like, it would be

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:21.667
super insulting if I didn't remember meeting Dennis Quaid.

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:22.834
-Right, right. -Yeah.

00:03:22.834 --> 00:03:24.534
-But, yeah, we --

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:26.901
I remember you called me when I just moved to New York.

00:03:26.901 --> 00:03:28.367
-Yeah.

00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:31.300
-And you said, "Hey, I know you're in 'Spider-Man.'

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.467
They hadn't announced what I was gonna be in "Spider-Man 3."

00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:35.334
And you said, "What are you playing?"

00:03:35.334 --> 00:03:37.100
And I knew you could keep a secret.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.334
So I said, "Playing Venom."

00:03:39.334 --> 00:03:41.467
And you were like, "Oh, it -- Hmm."

00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:44.033
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.033 --> 00:03:46.200
"He's kind of a bigger guy."

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.033
And I was like, "Oh, no, no. This is gonna work.

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:49.701
Like, they got a whole new take on it,

00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:51.567
and it'll be the most popular."

00:03:51.567 --> 00:03:53.467
-I was like, "So is it a cartoon now?

00:03:53.467 --> 00:03:57.067
Are they doing a -- Are you the voice of Venom?"

00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:59.601
[ Laughter ] -I actually had this idea.

00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:01.801
We couldn't make it work as an ad.

00:04:01.801 --> 00:04:04.767
'Cause "Home Economics" premiered literally like one day

00:04:04.767 --> 00:04:07.400
from when "Venom 2" was gonna premiere.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.901
And I was like, "We should do an ad."

00:04:09.901 --> 00:04:11.367
They just couldn't -- It was, like, Sony and Disney,

00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:13.033
and they couldn't figure it out --

00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:15.267
where all of our characters on "Home Economics"

00:04:15.267 --> 00:04:18.434
were sitting watching a -- I hope this is all --

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:21.334
We'll edit this out if it's not kosher.

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:23.834
But we were all watching the trailer for "Venom 2."

00:04:23.834 --> 00:04:27.701
And I just wanted my character to be, like, not into it.

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:30.033
So, I'm like, "Who even is this guy?"

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:32.801
They go, "Oh, he's so jacked. He looks great."

00:04:32.801 --> 00:04:34.434
I'm like, "I don't know, I don't know."

00:04:34.434 --> 00:04:36.133
Like, "Who is Tom Hardy?"

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:38.267
And they're like, "You know, 'Mad Max' and, like, 'Dunkirk.'"

00:04:38.267 --> 00:04:40.033
I'm like, "Yeah, but is he, like, a good actor?"

00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:42.200
And they'll, "Yeah, he's been nominated for an Academy Award."

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.667
I'm like, "Okay, but, like, comic-book villain?"

00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:46.901
And they're like, "Bane, yeah. He's, like, amazing!"

00:04:46.901 --> 00:04:49.801
[ Laughter ] -And you're like, "I don't know.

00:04:49.801 --> 00:04:53.000
I always pictured Venom as, like, 'That '70s Show.'"

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.033
Like, "I thought it was more of that vibe."

00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:57.734
-He should be like a svelte, like, a...

00:04:57.734 --> 00:05:00.133
-Little guy.

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.334
Hey, you just got to give answers,

00:05:02.334 --> 00:05:03.901
or ask questions, I should say,

00:05:03.901 --> 00:05:05.934
or give answers on "College Jeopardy!"

00:05:05.934 --> 00:05:07.634
-Right. -And I heard that this

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:08.801
was a big deal for your mom.

00:05:08.801 --> 00:05:10.534
I should note, this would be a huge deal

00:05:10.534 --> 00:05:12.634
if I appeared on "Jeopardy!" for my parents, who watch it.

00:05:12.634 --> 00:05:14.267
-Yes, but, Seth, you could be on "Jeopardy!"

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:17.100
I am so -- I can only play smart people.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.634
And that's basically what I did.

00:05:19.634 --> 00:05:22.400
They had, like, cue cards. We did, like, many takes.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.434
I had no idea what the answers were

00:05:25.434 --> 00:05:26.968
or what I was even talking about.

00:05:26.968 --> 00:05:28.868
But then I got to say I was on "Jeopardy!",

00:05:28.868 --> 00:05:31.067
And you look, like, really smart when you're on it.

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:33.467
-Yeah. And was -- Your mom was impressed?

00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:36.033
-Yeah, I think now she can say, "My son was on 'Jeopardy!'"

00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:38.200
-Well, that's great. And nobody asks any follow-ups

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.133
to get to the bottom of, like, what you were doing on it.

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:42.067
-No. -[ Laughter ]

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:44.033
-You got to -- Speaking of smart people,

00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:46.267
Mark Cuban did an episode of the show.

00:05:46.267 --> 00:05:47.467
-Yeah.

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:49.601
-You are a "Shark Tank" fan, correct?

00:05:49.601 --> 00:05:50.968
-I am the biggest "Shark Tank" --

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:52.968
I think most -- My wife's an actor, too,

00:05:52.968 --> 00:05:54.434
and we kind of watch it

00:05:54.434 --> 00:05:57.968
to feel like we understand business in some way,

00:05:57.968 --> 00:05:59.701
and we kind of chime in on, like,

00:05:59.701 --> 00:06:02.934
"I don't think this is a good deal for Barbara."

00:06:02.934 --> 00:06:06.133
But, like, Mark is incredible, and, you know,

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:09.267
such a great guy on that show, but we asked him to come do

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:11.000
"home Economics," and he said yes.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.701
And it's all in a poker-table scene.

00:06:12.701 --> 00:06:15.334
And so I got to spend, like, a couple of days with him,

00:06:15.334 --> 00:06:19.300
and I felt so good at business...

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:20.834
[ Laughter ] ...after the third day.

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.667
-That's the correct term. "Good at business."

00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:24.634
Yeah, that's how they say it. -No, he was so cool.

00:06:24.634 --> 00:06:26.834
He really was so cool.

00:06:26.834 --> 00:06:29.334
Like, surprisingly good at acting.

00:06:29.334 --> 00:06:31.801
-Yeah. -And Jimmy and I were like --

00:06:31.801 --> 00:06:34.601
At first, we were, like, gonna, try to give him some tips.

00:06:34.601 --> 00:06:36.901
By the end, he was, like, improvising, and it was good.

00:06:36.901 --> 00:06:39.000
Like, a lot of it made the show. And Jimmy and I were like,

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.634
"Why don't you stay in your lane, bro?"

00:06:40.634 --> 00:06:42.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:43.968
Like, "You can't have everything."

00:06:43.968 --> 00:06:45.701
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. "How about this?

00:06:45.701 --> 00:06:47.968
I won't seed-fund any new technology..."

00:06:47.968 --> 00:06:50.701
-"And you stay away from sitcoms."

00:06:50.701 --> 00:06:53.901
Jimmy has, like -- He's not as big a fan as I am,

00:06:53.901 --> 00:06:56.067
and he was kind of making fun of that I was a super fan.

00:06:56.067 --> 00:06:57.834
And then, to get under my skin,

00:06:57.834 --> 00:07:00.534
he started pitching all these products to Mark,

00:07:00.534 --> 00:07:03.300
and Mark was such a sweetheart, he really gave him feedback.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.701
But it was like Milwaukee Talkies,

00:07:05.701 --> 00:07:08.234
which are walkie-talkies that only work in Milwaukee.

00:07:08.234 --> 00:07:09.767
[ Laughter ]

00:07:09.767 --> 00:07:11.534
And Birken-socks was this thing.

00:07:11.534 --> 00:07:13.834
Like, if you want to wear socks but not Birkenstocks,

00:07:13.834 --> 00:07:16.467
the Birkenstocks are painted on the socks.

00:07:16.467 --> 00:07:18.734
-[ Laughter ] -And he was patient with this?

00:07:18.734 --> 00:07:21.000
-No, Mark was like, "I think, yeah, there's a place for that.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.534
Like, it might not be for me."

00:07:22.534 --> 00:07:24.334
[ Laughter ]

00:07:24.334 --> 00:07:27.000
-So he didn't jump in on Milwaukee Talkies?

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.167
-No, man.

00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:29.801
-I think he's gonna regret that one.

00:07:29.801 --> 00:07:31.167
-"He's gonna regret that, man!"

00:07:31.167 --> 00:07:32.934
-Hey, thanks for being here, buddy.

00:07:32.934 --> 00:07:35.667
It's always such a pleasure. You guys, that's Topher Grace.

00:07:35.667 --> 00:07:37.634
"Home Economics" airs Wednesdays on ABC.

